4.11 Not like assignment in C
A programming language would read the next two lines top to bottom:
assign b = a;
assign c = b;
First copy a into b, then copy b into c. In hardware both lines exist at the same time as two connections, and b is just the wire between them. A synthesis tool removes it and infers assign c = a. For the same reason, swapping the two lines changes nothing.

4.12 Two drivers on one net
The second example is the one that breaks C intuition completely:
logic c;
assign c = a;
assign c = b;
In a programming language the second line would overwrite the first, and c would end up with the value of b. In hardware, both lines are always active, so the net c is driven by two sources at once. When a and b agree, nothing is wrong. When they differ, one source pulls the wire high while the other pulls it low, and the simulator reports an unknown value X (Figure 4.2). In a real circuit, this is a short circuit between two gate outputs.






Figure 4.2: A net driven by a and by b: whenever they disagree, c is X.



If c were declared as logic, the tool would reject the second assignment at compile time, because a logic signal may have only one driver. That is the reason to prefer logic: it turns a silent X in simulation into an error you see immediately. This situation must be avoided in every design that is not a deliberate tri-state bus.

4.16 Conditional operator, concatenation and replication
The conditional operator cond ? x : y selects between two values. In hardware it is a multiplexer: both x and y exist, and cond chooses which one reaches the output. It can be nested, which is how we build larger multiplexers below.
The concatenation operator {} joins bits, vectors or expressions into one longer vector. The replication operator {n{...}} repeats a value n times:
logic [3:0] a = 4'b1010;
logic [3:0] b = 4'b0101;
logic [7:0] c;
logic [1:0] d = 2'b11;
logic [5:0] e;

assign c = {a, b};     // 8'b10100101
assign e = {3{d}};     // 6'b111111
Concatenation is also valid on the left of an assignment, which we use below to capture the carry of an adder.

4.18 Example: 4-bit adder
The adder adds two 4-bit numbers and produces a 4-bit sum and a carry-out bit. We build it exactly as on paper: a half adder for the least significant bit, then a full adder for each following bit, with an internal carry wire c1, c2, c3 passed from one stage to the next.
module adder4bit (
    input  logic [3:0] a, b,
    output logic [3:0] sum,
    output logic       carry_out
);
  logic c1, c2, c3;                       // carries between stages

  // Half adder: least significant bit
  assign sum[0] = a[0] ^ b[0];
  assign c1     = a[0] & b[0];

  // Full adders
  assign sum[1] = a[1] ^ b[1] ^ c1;
  assign c2     = (a[1] & b[1]) | (c1 & (a[1] ^ b[1]));

  assign sum[2] = a[2] ^ b[2] ^ c2;
  assign c3     = (a[2] & b[2]) | (c2 & (a[2] ^ b[2]));

  assign sum[3]    = a[3] ^ b[3] ^ c3;
  assign carry_out = (a[3] & b[3]) | (c3 & (a[3] ^ b[3]));
endmodule
Writing out the gate equations is useful once, to see what an adder is. In practice we let the tool do it. The + operator, with a concatenation on the left to catch the carry, describes the same adder in one line:
assign {carry_out, sum} = a + b;
The 5-bit result of a + b is split across the 1-bit carry_out and the 4-bit sum. Figure 4.4 shows the hand-built carry chain, and Figure 4.5 the single adder block that a synthesis tool is free to map onto the dedicated carry logic of an FPGA.






Figure 4.4: The gate-level adder4bit: a half adder followed by three full adders, with the carry rippling from bit to bit.









Figure 4.5: The same adder written with the + operator.

5.14 Blocking vs. non-blocking assignment
We have used two assignment operators in an always block, and the pairing has been the same every time: = in always_comb, and &lt;= in always_ff. Now we can see why.
A blocking assignment, =, takes effect immediately: the next statement in the block sees the new value. A non-blocking assignment, &lt;=, only schedules the update, which lands at the end of the current time step: the next statement still sees the old value. The difference only shows when there is interdependency, when a later statement reads something an earlier one wrote.

5.14.1 In combinational logic
Take three inputs and the function s = c ^ (a ^ b), written with an intermediate signal, once with each operator:
module xor_gate (
  input  logic a, b, c,
  output logic s1, s2
);
  logic n1, n2;

  // case 1: blocking
  always_comb begin
    n1 = a ^ b;
    s1 = c ^ n1;
  end

  // case 2: non-blocking
  always_comb begin
    n2 &lt;= a ^ b;
    s2 &lt;= c ^ n2;
  end
endmodule
(Some tools refuse non-blocking assignments inside always_comb altogether; the example is written this way only to show what would happen.)






Figure 5.14: The two XOR descriptions: s1 always matches c ^ n1, while s2 is computed from the previous value of n2.



Figure 5.14 shows the result for the same inputs. In case 1, n1 updates immediately, and the next line uses the new n1, so s1 is always c ^ n1. In case 2, n2 is only scheduled to change, so s2 &lt;= c ^ n2 reads the previous n2. Compare the last two rows: n changes at the start of columns 2 and 6, and at exactly those two points s2 differs from s1, because it still uses the old n. Case 1 models a combinational circuit; case 2 does not, because its output depends on a stale value. Synthesis tools often ignore the distinction in combinational blocks and build the right gates anyway, which makes it worse: the simulation shows one behavior and the hardware has another, a simulation/synthesis mismatch, the kind of bug that is hardest to find.


5.14.2 In sequential logic
The same experiment on two registers in a chain:
module chain (
  input  logic clk, a,
  output logic f1, f2
);
  logic b1, b2;

  // case 1: blocking
  always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
    b1 = a;
    f1 = b1;
  end

  // case 2: non-blocking
  always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
    b2 &lt;= a;
    f2 &lt;= b2;
  end
endmodule






Figure 5.15: Two registers in a chain: with blocking assignment f1 follows b1 in the same cycle, with non-blocking assignment f2 lags by one more clock.



In Figure 5.15 the clock is drawn with two columns per period, so it rises at the start of columns 1, 3, 5 and 7. The input a is high during columns 2 and 3, so the edge at the start of column 3 is the one that sees it. In case 1, b1 = a happens first, and f1 = b1 then reads the new b1, so f1 gets the value of a in the same cycle: both change at the edge starting column 3, and the second register adds nothing. In case 2, b2 &lt;= a and f2 &lt;= b2 are both evaluated before either update lands, so f2 takes the value b2 had before this edge. b2 rises at column 3, but f2 only at the next edge, at column 5, and it falls one edge after b2 does, at column 7. That is a two-stage chain, which is what two registers in a row should be. This is the behavior every pipeline relies on, and it is why the swap a &lt;= b; b &lt;= a; works with &lt;= and silently fails with =.


